2121
01:41:52,893 --> 01:41:54,258
- Hi.

2122
01:41:54,394 --> 01:41:56,259
- Excuse me, will you turn
the camera off, please?

2123
01:41:56,396 --> 01:41:59,263
- We're here to see Mr. Conaway.
- You have to turn the camera off

2124
01:41:59,399 --> 01:42:02,197
while you're in the building.
- Oh, okay, all right.

2125
01:42:02,269 --> 01:42:04,032
Okay, turn it off now.

2126
01:42:04,938 --> 01:42:07,202
[ One hour later]
- Hey, Michael.
- Hi, how are you?

2127
01:42:07,274 --> 01:42:09,765
- I'm Mary Lorenz. I'm director
of Media Relations for K-Mart.

2128
01:42:09,910 --> 01:42:12,105
- Oh, good. All right, good.
- How can I help you today?

2129
01:42:12,245 --> 01:42:15,112
- Well, I'm here today...
This is Richard Costaldo.

2130
01:42:15,248 --> 01:42:17,614
- Richard, nice to meet you.
- And this is Mark Taylor.

2131
01:42:17,751 --> 01:42:20,117
- Mark.
- And they're students

2132
01:42:20,253 --> 01:42:24,053
from Columbine High School.
They were shot at Columbine,

2133
01:42:24,191 --> 01:42:27,183
in the massacre,
with bullets from K-Mart.

2134
01:42:27,327 --> 01:42:30,023
- You came a long way.
All the way from Colorado.

2135
01:42:30,163 --> 01:42:34,065
- Yeah, I just...
I was thinking that...

2136
01:42:34,201 --> 01:42:36,066
since you stopped selling
the handguns and all,

2137
01:42:36,203 --> 01:42:40,071
it'd kind of make sense to stop
selling the bullets too.

2138
01:42:40,207 --> 01:42:43,074
- Our request is that you get rid
of nine-millimetre bullets

2139
01:42:43,210 --> 01:42:45,405
and that you don't sell them
in the store completely.

2140
01:42:45,545 --> 01:42:48,070
- We do carry... You probably
are aware of K-Mart -

2141
01:42:48,215 --> 01:42:50,581
hopefully, you're shoppers
at our stores -

2142
01:42:50,717 --> 01:42:54,585
that we do only carry, you know,
sporting firearms

2143
01:42:54,721 --> 01:42:59,590
and the accessories
that go with the hunting sport.

2144
01:42:59,726 --> 01:43:03,093
And we'll certainly take your
message to our chairman and CEO,

2145
01:43:03,230 --> 01:43:05,494
Chuck Conaway.
He's not here today.

2146
01:43:05,632 --> 01:43:08,192
- He's not here today?
- No. He's not here, actually,

2147
01:43:08,335 --> 01:43:10,895
this whole week.
- Not at all during the week?

2148
01:43:11,038 --> 01:43:13,905
Do you have a limit on the
number of bullets, ammunition,

2149
01:43:14,041 --> 01:43:15,906
that people can purchase?
- You know, I can't answer

2150
01:43:16,043 --> 01:43:18,102
these questions for you.
I'm not the merchandiser

2151
01:43:18,245 --> 01:43:19,644
who places those products
in our stores.

2152
01:43:19,780 --> 01:43:20,974
- Can we speak to that person?

2153
01:43:21,114 --> 01:43:23,173
- But I can get answers
to those questions for you.

2154
01:43:23,316 --> 01:43:25,807
If you leave your card, I could
get those answers for you.

2155
01:43:25,952 --> 01:43:27,419
- We don't want to leave a card.

2156
01:43:27,554 --> 01:43:29,715
The reason why
we can't take a card

2157
01:43:29,856 --> 01:43:31,756
and come back
is because Mark here,

2158
01:43:31,892 --> 01:43:34,759
he's got a K-Mart bullet
just an inch away... right?

2159
01:43:34,895 --> 01:43:36,760
- Yeah.
- From your aorta.

2160
01:43:36,897 --> 01:43:40,128
- In between my aorta and spine.
- Between your aorta and spine.

2161
01:43:40,267 --> 01:43:42,963
- I'm glad to see that
you're still able to stand.

2162
01:43:43,103 --> 01:43:45,571
- And I told him that somebody
here would listen,

2163
01:43:45,706 --> 01:43:48,072
somebody here would... would...

2164
01:43:48,208 --> 01:43:52,076
would take the request
seriously. Not just a PR person,

2165
01:43:52,212 --> 01:43:54,077
but somebody who has some
authority and can answer

2166
01:43:54,214 --> 01:43:56,648
some of the questions
that they want answered.

2167
01:43:56,783 --> 01:43:58,648
- K-Mart does care about this,

2168
01:43:58,785 --> 01:44:00,650
but I can't go any further
right now.

2169
01:44:00,787 --> 01:44:02,652
So until I make a call, um,

2170
01:44:02,789 --> 01:44:05,280
I'm gonna go back to the office

2171
01:44:05,425 --> 01:44:07,950
and see if there's anyone
in merchandising...

2172
01:44:08,095 --> 01:44:11,394
- Mary went back upstairs.
And two hours later,

2173
01:44:11,531 --> 01:44:14,398
she brought down this guy whose
job it is to buy the bullets

2174
01:44:14,534 --> 01:44:15,899
for K-Mart.

2175
01:44:16,036 --> 01:44:18,596
- Good. Stay out of trouble.
- Yes.

2176
01:44:18,739 --> 01:44:20,900
- We're not the ones in trouble,
guys.

2177
01:44:21,041 --> 01:44:23,100
Mark thought he'd show him
his bullet wounds.

2178
01:44:23,243 --> 01:44:25,040
Those are his bullet holes.
- Hm.

2179
01:44:25,178 --> 01:44:27,043
- From your bullets.

2180
01:44:27,180 --> 01:44:29,808
That's where the K-Mart bullets
went in.

2181
01:44:29,950 --> 01:44:31,815
- Well, take care.

2182
01:44:31,952 --> 01:44:34,546
- Is anybody else gonna
come down?

2183
01:44:34,688 --> 01:44:36,553
Is anybody else gonna come down?
Is that it?

2184
01:44:36,690 --> 01:44:38,920
- I'll check.
- Okay, thank you.

2185
01:44:39,059 --> 01:44:41,550
We waited around
a couple more hours

2186
01:44:41,695 --> 01:44:43,822
but no one else came down.

2187
01:44:43,964 --> 01:44:46,933
As we left the building,
Mark came up with an idea.

2188
01:44:47,067 --> 01:44:48,932
He suggested that we go

2189
01:44:49,069 --> 01:44:53,836
to the nearest K-Mart
and buy out all their bullets.

2190
01:44:53,974 --> 01:44:56,772
- Just take as many of those
as you can.

2191
01:44:56,910 --> 01:44:59,276
- Yeah, you can come around here
and look.

2192
01:44:59,412 --> 01:45:02,006
- What else do we have over here?

2193
01:45:02,149 --> 01:45:05,016
You got 357. Sure, I'll take 'em
all, take everything you got.

2194
01:45:05,152 --> 01:45:07,586
- So you're 17? You're what?
- Sixteen.

2195
01:45:07,721 --> 01:45:09,086
- Wait.

2196
01:45:09,222 --> 01:45:11,087
- Oh, shit!

2197
01:45:11,224 --> 01:45:13,522
- Oh, my God...

2198
01:45:13,660 --> 01:45:16,595
- Mark pretty much cleaned them
out of their ammunition.

2199
01:45:16,730 --> 01:45:19,961
And the next day, we decided to
go back to K-Mart headquarters

2200
01:45:20,100 --> 01:45:22,125
with all the bullets.
This time,

2201
01:45:22,302 --> 01:45:23,792
we brought the press.

2202
01:45:24,004 --> 01:45:25,335
- Our local first coverage
of south-eastern Michigan

2203
01:45:25,505 --> 01:45:27,029
continues now
with all-new stories.

2204
01:45:29,943 --> 01:45:31,968
- Coming up here on
our six-o'clock report,

2205
01:45:32,112 --> 01:45:33,841
a warning to everyone this
summer to watch out for snakes.

2206
01:45:33,947 --> 01:45:36,916
You'll hear from a mom who was
bitten by a rattlesnake.

2207
01:45:37,050 --> 01:45:38,915
- And also, students who survived
the Columbine massacre

2208
01:45:39,052 --> 01:45:42,544
are in town.
They are very angry with K-Mart.

2209
01:45:42,689 --> 01:45:45,658
- We're here to see Chuck
Conaway, the chairman of K-Mart.

2210
01:45:45,792 --> 01:45:47,555
- How you doing, sir?
It's always a pleasure.

2211
01:45:47,694 --> 01:45:51,061
Okay, uh...

2212
01:45:51,198 --> 01:45:54,099
They would like to speak
to Mr. Conaway.

2213
01:45:54,234 --> 01:45:58,000
- Here's the nine-millimetres.

2214
01:45:58,138 --> 01:46:00,663
These are the bullets
that are in both Richard

2215
01:46:00,807 --> 01:46:03,105
and in Mark's body right now.

2216
01:46:10,650 --> 01:46:11,674
- Move your group outside.

2217
01:46:11,818 --> 01:46:13,718
I'll have somebody here
in five minutes.

2218
01:46:13,854 --> 01:46:15,378
- Do me a favour,
don't block the door.

2219
01:46:15,522 --> 01:46:17,046
Just off to the side,
if you would.

2220
01:46:17,190 --> 01:46:18,748
- Will go outside and
somebody will come out.

2221
01:46:18,892 --> 01:46:19,950
- My name's Laurie McTavish.

2222
01:46:20,093 --> 01:46:22,653
I'm the Vice-president of
Communications for K-Mart.

2223
01:46:22,796 --> 01:46:26,027
I'm happy to deliver a statement
on behalf of the company.

2224
01:46:26,166 --> 01:46:29,533
What happened in Columbine,
Colorado, was truly tragic

2225
01:46:29,669 --> 01:46:31,534
and touched every American.

2226
01:46:31,671 --> 01:46:34,037
We're sorry for the...

2227
01:46:34,174 --> 01:46:36,233
disadvantage to this young man.

2228
01:46:36,376 --> 01:46:39,038
K-Mart is phasing out the sale
of handgun ammunition.

2229
01:46:39,179 --> 01:46:42,478
The business plan calls
for this to be complete

2230
01:46:42,616 --> 01:46:46,552
in the continental U.S.
within the next 90 days.

2231
01:46:46,686 --> 01:46:48,051
- Wow! Wow!

2232
01:46:48,188 --> 01:46:50,053
- K-Mart representatives met
with Mr. Moore

2233
01:46:50,190 --> 01:46:52,556
and the students from Columbine,
Colorado, yesterday,

2234
01:46:52,692 --> 01:46:55,559
and listened to their concerns
about the product

2235
01:46:55,695 --> 01:46:57,560
carried in K-Mart stores.

2236
01:46:57,697 --> 01:47:00,564
The company committed,
at the end of that meeting,

2237
01:47:00,700 --> 01:47:04,431
that K-Mart would have an answer
for them within a week's time.

2238
01:47:04,571 --> 01:47:07,438
- Well, the first thing we want
to do is thank you

2239
01:47:07,574 --> 01:47:11,066
for committing to no longer
selling handgun ammunition

2240
01:47:11,211 --> 01:47:13,076
in your stores.
And within 90 days--

2241
01:47:13,213 --> 01:47:15,579
- The process will be phased out
within 90 days.

2242
01:47:15,715 --> 01:47:18,081
- And after 90 days,
there will be no more selling

2243
01:47:18,218 --> 01:47:21,085
of ammunition that can go into
handguns or assault weapons.

2244
01:47:21,221 --> 01:47:23,416
- Firearm ammunition, will be...
We will not sell it,

2245
01:47:23,556 --> 01:47:25,421
after 90 days, in our stores.

2246
01:47:25,558 --> 01:47:28,356
- We greatly appreciate that.
- Thank you.

2247
01:47:28,495 --> 01:47:30,554
- Thank you very much. Thank you.
That's very brave.

2248
01:47:33,667 --> 01:47:35,328
Thank you. Wow!

2249
01:47:35,468 --> 01:47:38,699
That blows my mind. That's
more than what we asked for.

2250
01:47:38,838 --> 01:47:41,534
- It's remarkable.
- Yeah. Well, like I told you--

2251
01:47:41,675 --> 01:47:43,540
- I didn't think--
- Did you think?

2252
01:47:43,677 --> 01:47:45,542
- No!
- We're like, uh...

2253
01:47:45,679 --> 01:47:49,775
We're just getting ready to...
We're going to the airport.

2254
01:47:49,916 --> 01:47:52,544
The kids from Columbine had
scored an overwhelming victory

2255
01:47:52,686 --> 01:47:54,551
against K-Mart
and it inspired me

2256
01:47:54,688 --> 01:47:58,784
to do something that
I knew I had to do.

2257
01:47:58,925 --> 01:48:01,485
All I needed... was a star map.

2258
01:48:06,700 --> 01:48:09,066
[ ringing gate bell ]

2259
01:48:09,202 --> 01:48:11,102
[ phone ringing ]

2260
01:48:22,215 --> 01:48:24,012
- Hello?
- Mr. Heston?

2261
01:48:24,150 --> 01:48:26,015
- Yeah.
- This is Michael Moore.

2262
01:48:26,152 --> 01:48:27,517
- Yes.
- The film-maker?

2263
01:48:27,654 --> 01:48:29,519
- Yes, of course.
- Yes. How you doing?

2264
01:48:29,656 --> 01:48:31,021
- Fine, thank you.

2265
01:48:31,157 --> 01:48:34,024
- Listen, I was wondering
if maybe I could talk to you.

2266
01:48:34,160 --> 01:48:37,857
We're making a documentary
about the whole gun issue.

2267
01:48:37,998 --> 01:48:39,863
And I'm a member of the NRA.

2268
01:48:40,000 --> 01:48:42,525
I thought maybe we could talk
a little bit about--

2269
01:48:42,669 --> 01:48:44,534
- Tell you what, let me look
at my calendar.

2270
01:48:44,671 --> 01:48:47,037
I may be able to give you
some time tomorrow.

2271
01:48:47,173 --> 01:48:50,040
I have some people here now.
- Okay, well, how can I--

2272
01:48:50,176 --> 01:48:51,541
Pardon me?
- Hold the phone.

2273
01:48:51,678 --> 01:48:53,270
- Okay, thank you.
- Okay.

2274
01:48:53,413 --> 01:48:56,041
I can give you a little time
tomorrow morning.

2275
01:48:56,182 --> 01:48:58,047
I think that's Thursday.
- Yes.

2276
01:48:58,184 --> 01:49:00,550
- Let's say 8:30.
- Eight-thirty in the morning?

2277
01:49:00,687 --> 01:49:02,552
- Yeah. Okay?
- Okay. And just come here?

2278
01:49:02,689 --> 01:49:05,351
- Yes.
- Okay, good.

2279
01:49:08,661 --> 01:49:10,561
[ phone ringing ]
- Hello?

2280
01:49:10,697 --> 01:49:13,063
- Hi. It's Michael Moore here
to see Charlton Heston.

2281
01:49:13,199 --> 01:49:15,099
- Okay.
[ beeping ]

2282
01:49:19,205 --> 01:49:21,105
[ theme music from
"Mr. Roger's Neighborhood" ]

2283
01:49:34,721 --> 01:49:37,121
- Hi. Good morning.

2284
01:49:42,228 --> 01:49:44,594
How are you?
- Fine.

2285
01:49:44,731 --> 01:49:46,995
- Thank you very much
for agreeing to see me.

2286
01:49:55,241 --> 01:49:57,607
He took me out
to his pool-and-tennis house

2287
01:49:57,744 --> 01:49:59,609
so we could have a chat.

2288
01:49:59,746 --> 01:50:02,681
I told him that I was
a lifetime member of the NRA

2289
01:50:02,816 --> 01:50:04,943
and showed him
my membership card.

2290
01:50:05,085 --> 01:50:06,882
- Good for you. Well done.

2291
01:50:07,020 --> 01:50:08,885
- I assume you have guns
in the house here?

2292
01:50:09,022 --> 01:50:12,116
- Indeed I do.
Bad guys take notice!

2293
01:50:12,258 --> 01:50:14,123
- So you have them
for protection?

2294
01:50:14,260 --> 01:50:16,125
- Yeah. Sure.
- Have you ever been

2295
01:50:16,262 --> 01:50:18,127
a victim of crime?
- No. No.

2296
01:50:18,264 --> 01:50:22,030
- Never been assaulted or...?
- No.

2297
01:50:22,168 --> 01:50:24,966
- No violence toward you,
but you have guns in the house.

2298
01:50:25,105 --> 01:50:27,073
- Loaded.
- They're loaded?

2299
01:50:27,207 --> 01:50:29,573
- Well, if you really needed
a weapon for self-defence,

2300
01:50:29,709 --> 01:50:31,574
you need it loaded.
- Okay, but why...

2301
01:50:31,711 --> 01:50:33,576
why do you need it for
self-defence? Because--

2302
01:50:33,713 --> 01:50:35,578
- I don't.
- Yeah, you've never been

2303
01:50:35,715 --> 01:50:37,580
a victim of crime,
you haven't been assaulted.

2304
01:50:37,717 --> 01:50:39,582
- No, that's true.
- You haven't been, you know...

2305
01:50:39,719 --> 01:50:43,086
Why would you... So why not...
Why don't you unload the gun?

2306
01:50:43,223 --> 01:50:46,590
- Because the second amendment
gives me the right

2307
01:50:46,726 --> 01:50:48,591
to have it loaded.
- I agree.

2308
01:50:48,728 --> 01:50:51,094
I totally agree with that.
I'm just saying... I mean,

2309
01:50:51,231 --> 01:50:52,596
the second amendment gives me--

2310
01:50:52,732 --> 01:50:54,597
- Let's say it's
a comfort factor.

2311
01:50:54,734 --> 01:50:57,601
- It gives you comfort to know
that there's a loaded gun.

2312
01:50:57,737 --> 01:50:59,102
- Yeah.

2313
01:50:59,239 --> 01:51:02,106
- Comfort meaning that it allows
you to relax and feel safe?

2314
01:51:02,242 --> 01:51:04,107
- Not worry about it.
- Not worry, not be afraid.

2315
01:51:04,244 --> 01:51:06,041
- And I'm not really, but, uh...

2316
01:51:06,179 --> 01:51:10,616
I'm exercising one of the rights
passed on down to me

2317
01:51:10,750 --> 01:51:13,617
from those wise, old, dead white
guys that invented this country.

2318
01:51:13,753 --> 01:51:16,722
If it was good enough for them,
it's good enough for me.

2319
01:51:16,856 --> 01:51:18,517
- But you could still
exercise the right,

2320
01:51:18,658 --> 01:51:21,092
just by having a gun unloaded
and locked away somewhere.

2321
01:51:21,227 --> 01:51:22,387
- I choose to have it.

2322
01:51:22,529 --> 01:51:25,054
- What sort of strikes me
as interesting is that,

2323
01:51:25,198 --> 01:51:28,065
in other countries, where
they don't have the murder rate,

2324
01:51:28,201 --> 01:51:30,066
the gun-murder rate
that we have, that, uh...

2325
01:51:30,203 --> 01:51:32,068
many people say,

2326
01:51:32,205 --> 01:51:34,070
"Well, that's because
they don't have guns around.

2327
01:51:34,207 --> 01:51:37,074
It's hard to get a gun in
Britain or Germany or whatever."

2328
01:51:37,210 --> 01:51:41,078
But we went to Canada and
there's seven million guns

2329
01:51:41,214 --> 01:51:43,205
in 10-million homes.
- There won't be very long.

2330
01:51:43,349 --> 01:51:44,873
- But hear me out, though.
- Okay.

2331
01:51:45,018 --> 01:51:46,576
- Canada is a nation of hunters,

2332
01:51:46,719 --> 01:51:48,584
millions of guns,

2333
01:51:48,721 --> 01:51:51,588
and yet, they had just
a few murders last year.

2334
01:51:51,724 --> 01:51:53,589
That's it. A country
of 30-million people.

2335
01:51:53,726 --> 01:51:56,593
Now, why - here's my question -
why is it that...

2336
01:51:56,729 --> 01:51:58,720
that they've got all these guns
laying around,

2337
01:51:58,865 --> 01:52:00,730
yet they don't kill each other

2338
01:52:00,867 --> 01:52:02,732
at the level that
we kill each other?

2339
01:52:02,869 --> 01:52:05,099
- I think American history is...

2340
01:52:05,238 --> 01:52:07,604
uh... has a lot of blood

2341
01:52:07,740 --> 01:52:09,605
on its hands.
- And Germany history doesn't?

2342
01:52:09,742 --> 01:52:11,607
- No.
- And British history?

2343
01:52:11,744 --> 01:52:14,110
- I don't think as much.
- Oh, are you...

2344
01:52:14,247 --> 01:52:16,613
Germans don't have as much,
blood on their hands?

2345
01:52:16,749 --> 01:52:19,115
- Uh, they do, yes.
- The Brits, they ruled the world

2346
01:52:19,252 --> 01:52:21,618
for 300 years
at the barrel of a gun.

2347
01:52:21,754 --> 01:52:25,053
They're all violent people. They
have bad guys, they have crime,

2348
01:52:25,191 --> 01:52:27,557
they have lots of guns--

2349
01:52:27,694 --> 01:52:31,562
- Well, it's an interesting
point, which can be explored

2350
01:52:31,698 --> 01:52:35,065
and you're good to explore it
at great lengths,

2351
01:52:35,201 --> 01:52:38,068
but I think that's about all
I have to say on it.

2352
01:52:38,204 --> 01:52:41,071
- You don't have any opinion,
though, as to why that is,

2353
01:52:41,207 --> 01:52:43,072
that we are the unique country,
the only country,

2354
01:52:43,209 --> 01:52:46,076
that does this, that kills each
other on this level, with guns?

2355
01:52:46,212 --> 01:52:50,080
- Well, we have, probably,
more mixed ethnicity

2356
01:52:50,216 --> 01:52:53,583
than other countries,
some other countries.

2357
01:52:53,720 --> 01:52:55,585
- You think it's an ethnic thing?

2358
01:52:55,722 --> 01:52:57,587
- No, I don't. It's...

2359
01:52:57,724 --> 01:52:59,589
I wouldn't go so far
as to say that.

2360
01:52:59,726 --> 01:53:03,093
We had enough problems with
civil rights in the beginning.

2361
01:53:03,229 --> 01:53:07,097
It's... But, uh, I have
no answer for that.

2362
01:53:07,233 --> 01:53:09,599
- What do you mean, you think
it's a mixed ethnicity?

2363
01:53:09,736 --> 01:53:11,601
I don't understand.

2364
01:53:11,738 --> 01:53:13,603
- You said, "How is it that..."
- That we're unique.

2365
01:53:13,740 --> 01:53:17,608
- "So many Americans
kill each other?"

2366
01:53:17,744 --> 01:53:20,008
I don't know that that's true,
but, uh...

2367
01:53:20,146 --> 01:53:21,511
- Well, no, you know that.

2368
01:53:21,648 --> 01:53:24,549
We know we have the highest
murder rate with guns.

2369
01:53:24,684 --> 01:53:26,549
It's way higher
than any other country.

2370
01:53:26,686 --> 01:53:30,053
- The only answer I can give you
is the one I already gave you.

2371
01:53:30,189 --> 01:53:33,056
- Which is?
- Which is that we have, uh...

2372
01:53:33,192 --> 01:53:36,059
- Historically--
- ...a history of violence.

2373
01:53:36,195 --> 01:53:39,562
Perhaps more than most
countries. Not more than Russia,

2374
01:53:39,699 --> 01:53:42,566
not more than Japan or China--
- Not more than Germany.

2375
01:53:42,702 --> 01:53:47,196
- Not more than Germany,
but certainly more than Canada.

2376
01:53:47,340 --> 01:53:50,070
- I come from Flint, Michigan,
and last year,

2377
01:53:50,209 --> 01:53:53,076
a little six-year-old boy
took a gun into a classroom

2378
01:53:53,212 --> 01:53:56,181
and shot and killed a
six-year-old girl. And, uh...

2379
01:53:56,316 --> 01:53:58,511
it was really a tragic thing--

2380
01:53:58,651 --> 01:54:01,620
- This was a kid, though.
- A six-year-old, yeah.

2381
01:54:01,754 --> 01:54:03,187
Did you hear about this?

2382
01:54:03,323 --> 01:54:05,985
A six-year-old shooting
a six-year-old?

2383
01:54:06,125 --> 01:54:07,558
- Yeah.
- Well, here's my question.

2384
01:54:07,694 --> 01:54:08,592
After that happened,

2385
01:54:08,728 --> 01:54:10,593
you came to Flint
and held a big rally.

2386
01:54:10,730 --> 01:54:12,095
- Mm-hm.

2387
01:54:12,231 --> 01:54:14,597
- And, you know, I just--

2388
01:54:14,734 --> 01:54:17,100
- So did the vice-president.

2389
01:54:17,236 --> 01:54:20,103
- Yeah, but did you feel it was
being at all insensitive

2390
01:54:20,239 --> 01:54:22,605
to the fact that this community
had just gone through--

2391
01:54:22,742 --> 01:54:25,540
- Actually, I wasn't aware
of that at the time we came.

2392
01:54:25,678 --> 01:54:29,045
We came and did an
early-morning, uh... rally,

2393
01:54:29,182 --> 01:54:31,047
then went on to wherever
we were going.

2394
01:54:31,184 --> 01:54:34,051
- You didn't know at the time,
that this killing had happened?

2395
01:54:34,187 --> 01:54:36,052
- No.
- Had you known, would you have--

2396
01:54:36,189 --> 01:54:38,054
- Would I have cancelled
the... uh...

2397
01:54:38,191 --> 01:54:40,056
- Yeah.
- I don't...

2398
01:54:40,193 --> 01:54:41,558
Hard to say.

2399
01:54:41,694 --> 01:54:43,559
- It wasn't like it was
already planned.

2400
01:54:43,696 --> 01:54:45,561
I mean, the choice to come there
was made

2401
01:54:45,698 --> 01:54:47,563
after this horrible
killing took place.

2402
01:54:47,700 --> 01:54:49,065
- Yeah. Mm-hm.

2403
01:54:49,202 --> 01:54:52,069
- You know, had you know that,
would you have come?

2404
01:54:52,205 --> 01:54:55,072
- I don't know. I have no idea.

2405
01:54:55,208 --> 01:54:57,199
- Maybe not. Maybe not.
- Okay. Thank you.

2406
01:54:57,343 --> 01:55:00,574
- You think you'd like to just
maybe apologize to the people

2407
01:55:00,713 --> 01:55:03,580
in Flint for coming and doing
that at that time, or...?

2408
01:55:03,716 --> 01:55:05,581
- You want me to apologize...

2409
01:55:05,718 --> 01:55:07,583
me, apologize to the people
in Flint?

2410
01:55:07,720 --> 01:55:09,620
- Or the people in Columbine

2411
01:55:09,756 --> 01:55:12,088
for coming after
their horrible tragedy.

2412
01:55:12,225 --> 01:55:14,591
Why do you go to the places
after they have

2413
01:55:14,727 --> 01:55:17,093
these horrible tragedies? I'm
a member of your group here--

2414
01:55:17,230 --> 01:55:20,097
- Well, I'm afraid
we don't agree on... on that.

2415
01:55:20,233 --> 01:55:22,599
- You think it's okay
to just come and show up

2416
01:55:22,735 --> 01:55:25,033
at these events.
- No.

2417
01:55:25,171 --> 01:55:27,071
- You don't think it's okay?

2418
01:55:38,084 --> 01:55:40,780
Mr. Heston, just one more thing.

2419
01:55:40,920 --> 01:55:42,785
This is who she is -

2420
01:55:42,922 --> 01:55:45,516
or was. This is her.

2421
01:55:45,658 --> 01:55:47,523
Mr. Heston, please don't leave.

2422
01:55:47,660 --> 01:55:50,356
Mr. Heston, please,
take a look at her.

2423
01:55:50,496 --> 01:55:53,090
This is the girl.

2424
01:56:44,150 --> 01:56:47,551
I left the Heston estate
atop Beverly Hills

2425
01:56:47,687 --> 01:56:49,655
and walked back
into the real world,

2426
01:56:49,789 --> 01:56:53,020
an America living
and breathing in fear...

2427
01:56:53,159 --> 01:56:55,059
In your mind,
you imagine somebody

2428
01:56:55,194 --> 01:56:58,061
who might break into your house,
to harm you or your family.

2429
01:56:58,197 --> 01:57:01,564
What does that person look like?

2430
01:57:01,701 --> 01:57:04,534
- You. Her. Him.
The camera guy, anybody.

2431
01:57:04,670 --> 01:57:07,036
Could be a gun in the camera,
I don't know.

2432
01:57:07,173 --> 01:57:09,835
- Where gun sales were now
at an all-time high...

2433
01:57:09,976 --> 01:57:13,070
- Can shoot as fast as
Wesson semi-automatic.

2434
01:57:13,212 --> 01:57:15,180
- And where, in the end,

2435
01:57:15,314 --> 01:57:17,373
it all comes back to bowling

2436
01:57:17,517 --> 01:57:19,382
for Columbine.

2437
01:57:19,519 --> 01:57:22,044
- Three bowling-alley employees
shot to death Sunday night

2438
01:57:22,188 --> 01:57:23,587
at the AMF Broadway Lanes.

2439
01:57:23,723 --> 01:57:26,521
- There's nothing I really know.
I really don't know anything.

2440
01:57:26,659 --> 01:57:29,184
- Just that three people died.
- Right.

2441
01:57:29,328 --> 01:57:30,852
- In Littleton,
in a bowling alley.

2442
01:57:30,997 --> 01:57:32,555
I'm sorry.

2443
01:57:34,700 --> 01:57:37,965
Yes, it was a glorious time
to be an American.

